== INTERPRETATION ==

- The pure deny-check functions correctly return a HardDeny descriptor for each of the four
  fake forbidden command inputs and three fake forbidden path inputs, and correctly return
  null for the harmless baselines.
- The denial decision is data-driven from a single registry (HARD_DENIES) consumed by both
  the bash exec wrapper (capability-enforcement.ts:50-56) and the FS Read/Write/Edit wrappers
  (capability-enforcement.ts:93-...), so a worker process cannot run, read, write, or edit a
  denied target through the standard tool surface — the wrapper returns exit code 126 and a
  `Denied: ...` message before any side effect occurs.

== SAFE README WORDING ==

OK to say: "Hard-deny rules block dangerous commands and sensitive file paths at the worker
tool boundary."
OK to say: "Token-aware deny matcher; harmless near-misses (e.g. echoing the word `sudo`,
a filename containing `deploy`) are not flagged."
OK to say: "Workers cannot bypass deny by piping, command substitution, shell -c, or inline
python/node/perl/ruby -c snippets."

== UNSAFE WORDING TO AVOID ==

Avoid: "Sandbox" — this is a deny list at the tool boundary, not a kernel sandbox.
Avoid: "Cannot be bypassed" / "uncircumventable" — this is a best-effort token-aware filter;
recursion depth is bounded at 8 and a worker invoking subprocesses outside the wrapped bash
tool (e.g. via a non-injected child_process import in vendored code) is not covered.
Avoid promising any specific exit code or message wording in the README.

STATUS: verified
REASON: The pure deny-check functions are exported from src/goal/hard-deny.ts and wired into
the worker tool surface at src/goal/capability-enforcement.ts:50-56 and 93-...; a safe in-process
probe of `checkCommandDeny`/`checkPathDeny` on four fake forbidden commands and three fake
forbidden paths returned a HardDeny match for every dangerous input and null for every
harmless baseline (9/9 PASS, exit 0). No dangerous command was executed.
